insert into public.posts (slug, title, excerpt, cover, body, tags, reading_minutes, published, published_at) values ('never-store-a-balance', 'Never store a balance', 'Why every wallet I build now uses a double-entry ledger, and the five lines of code that make it work.', '{"src":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551650975-87deedd944c3","width":4843,"height":3632,"alt":"Hand holding a phone with a finance app","credit":"Unsplash"}'::jsonb, 'The first wallet I built stored the balance as a number on the user row. It worked until two requests updated it at the same time, and a customer was briefly richer than they should have been.

## Balances are a query, not a column

In a double-entry ledger, every movement of money writes two entries that sum to zero. A balance is never stored. It is calculated from the entries.

```sql
select coalesce(sum(amount), 0) as balance
from ledger_entries
where account_id = $1;
```

That one decision removes a whole class of bugs. You cannot forget to update the balance, because there is nothing to update.

## What you get for free

- **An audit trail.** Every change has a reference, a time and a counterpart.
- **Reconciliation.** Totals per account should match the payment provider. When they do not, you know exactly which day to look at.
- **Safe retries.** Make the reference unique and a replayed webhook simply fails to insert.

## When it is overkill

If nothing in your product holds value between transactions, you do not need this. The moment users can top up, hold or withdraw, you do.', array['Fintech', 'Databases']::text[], 4, true, '2026-08-14T09:00:00.000Z');
insert into public.posts (slug, title, excerpt, cover, body, tags, reading_minutes, published, published_at) values ('a-dashboard-is-a-decision', 'A dashboard is a decision, not a chart', 'Most dashboards fail before the first query. Start with the decision someone needs to make on Monday morning.', '{"src":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1526628953301-3e589a6a8b74","width":2947,"height":2121,"alt":"Dashboard tiles with key metrics","credit":"Unsplash"}'::jsonb, 'I have built dashboards nobody opened. They were accurate, fast and quite pretty. They just did not help anyone decide anything.

## Start from the meeting

Before touching data, I ask one question: **what decision will this help you make, and when?** If the answer is "the Monday ops meeting, deciding which routes need more riders", the dashboard has a clear job.

## Agree on definitions first

Revenue, active customer, delivered order. Every team has a slightly different meaning for each. Write the definitions down, get them signed off, then encode them once in SQL views that every chart reuses.

## Fewer charts, clearer owners

A good dashboard has a handful of numbers and a named person who acts on each one. Everything else belongs in an ad-hoc report.', array['Data', 'BI']::text[], 3, true, '2026-06-02T09:00:00.000Z');
insert into public.posts (slug, title, excerpt, cover, body, tags, reading_minutes, published, published_at) values ('shipping-with-ai-in-the-loop', 'Shipping with AI in the loop', 'How I use coding agents day to day without losing track of what the code actually does.', '{"src":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1677442136019-21780ecad995","width":5120,"height":2880,"alt":"Abstract render of an AI processor","credit":"Unsplash"}'::jsonb, 'AI agents write a meaningful share of my code now. They have not made me less careful. They have changed where the care goes.

## Plans before prompts

For anything bigger than a small fix, I write a short plan first: the goal, the files involved, what done looks like. The agent works from the plan, and the plan becomes documentation.

## Small, verifiable steps

I ask for one change at a time and run the type checker, tests and build after each. An agent that can verify its own work is far more useful than one that writes a lot of code quickly.

## I still read every diff

Generated code is still my code. If I cannot explain a change in review, it does not ship.

## Where it shines

Boilerplate, migrations between library versions, test scaffolding and first drafts of admin screens. Where it needs the most supervision: security boundaries, money and anything involving time zones.', array['AI', 'Process']::text[], 4, true, '2026-09-20T09:00:00.000Z');

